---
/**
 * Layer 1 of the insurance analyser: the verdict, and the two numbers it rests
 * on. Rewritten 18 Aug for the overhaul.
 *
 * The single comparison this whole subject reduces to (dd-005) is: keep the
 * policy, or buy the cover on its own and invest the difference? Everything
 * else on this page is a push on one side of it.
 *
 * The design note that matters is in `InsuranceAnswer.checks.md`: the replica
 * is DEFINED to fund the identical income in the identical years, so the income
 * is held constant by construction and the only thing left to compare is what
 * remains at the end. That is said on the screen, in those words — without it a
 * reader cannot tell whether the surplus was bought by cutting their income.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED IN THE OVERHAUL
 *
 *  - THE RATE IS SAID IN THE READER'S WORDS. "TRUE YIELD (XIRR)" was F-13's
 *    finding and it led the old page. What a reader wants to know is what the
 *    policy pays them back a year, after tax, and that is what it now says.
 *
 *  - THE TAX-FREE LINE IS NEW, and it is the policy's genuine advantage. Where
 *    s.10(10D) exempts the proceeds, the policy's rate is worth more than the
 *    same rate from a bond, and almost nothing else on the internet will tell a
 *    policyholder that. Where the exemption is LOST, the same line says so.
 *    dd-021: a tool willing to print only one of those two is advocacy.
 *
 *  - F-12 / dd-019. Every figure names what it counts on the same line as the
 *    amount, or sits under a column header that frames it. "THE SAME MONEY" is
 *    gone — it read as a claim of equivalence when it meant "this sum,
 *    re-expressed".
 *
 * Every figure here is rendered by insurance-analyzer.astro from the one
 * `analyseReplication` result. This component holds no state and reads no form.
 */
---

<section
  id="insuranceAnswer"
  class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-xs"
  aria-live="polite"
>
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b border-navy-100 dark:border-navy-800 pb-4 mb-6">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
      <span class="inline-block w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-navy-400 dark:bg-navy-500"></span>
      <h2 class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-300">
        Policy Arbitrage Verdict
      </h2>
    </div>
    <span
      id="answerBadge"
      class="shrink-0 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-bold tracking-tight rounded-md bg-navy-100 dark:bg-navy-800 text-navy-700 dark:text-navy-300 border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-700/60"
    >
      Working it out
    </span>
  </div>

  <!--
    dd-008: the values live in fixed slots the eye can return to, and the prose
    around them stays true at every position of every input. Nothing here is
    rewritten as a control moves; only the slots change.
  -->
  <p
    id="answerHeadline"
    class="text-lg sm:text-2xl font-bold leading-snug text-navy-950 dark:text-white"
  >
    Tell us about the policy and this will say which way the arithmetic goes.
  </p>

  <!--
    The premise the comparison rests on. Fixed text on purpose: it is a
    principle, not a state (dd-008), and without it the figures below are
    unreadable — the reader has no way to know the surplus was not bought by
    cutting their own income.
  -->
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">
    Both routes pay you <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100"
      >the same money, at the same times</strong
    >, and after the same taxes. The do-it-yourself route is built to fund every rupee this
    policy hands you, so the only things left to compare are what is left over at the end and
    what the protection costs to keep.
  </p>

  <!--
    T8 / dd-004 / dd-019. One quantity, two moneys, adjacent columns with the
    frame in the header and the quantity in the row.
  -->
  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-navy-100 dark:border-navy-800/80 bg-navy-50/40 dark:bg-navy-900/30 p-1">
    <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-navy-200/80 dark:border-navy-800">
          <th class="py-2.5 px-3 text-xs font-semibold text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">Core Metric</th>
          <th class="py-2.5 px-3 text-right text-xs font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
            In rupees of that year
          </th>
          <th class="py-2.5 px-3 text-right text-xs font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
            In today's prices
          </th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-navy-100 dark:divide-navy-800/60">
        <tr>
          <th
            id="answerFigureLabel"
            scope="row"
            class="py-3.5 px-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100 align-baseline"
          >Left over at the end</th>
          <td class="py-3.5 px-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerFigureNominal" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-950 dark:text-white">₹0</span>
          </td>
          <td class="py-3.5 px-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerFigureReal" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-950 dark:text-white">₹0</span>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="py-3.5 px-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100 align-baseline">
            What the same protection costs to buy on its own
          </th>
          <td class="py-3.5 px-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerCoverCost" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-950 dark:text-white">₹0</span>
          </td>
          <td class="py-3.5 px-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerCoverCostReal" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-950 dark:text-white">₹0</span>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p id="answerCoverNote" class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">&nbsp;</p>

  <!--
    THE RATE, in the reader's words: Clear post-tax XIRR with tax notes.
  -->
  <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-navy-50/80 dark:bg-navy-900/60 p-5 space-y-2">
    <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
        What this policy pays you back, a year, after tax
      </p>
      <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">NET XIRR</span>
    </div>
    <p class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-950 dark:text-white">
      <span id="answerYield">0.00%</span>
    </p>
    <p id="answerYieldNote" class="text-sm text-navy-700 dark:text-navy-300 leading-relaxed font-medium">&nbsp;</p>
    <p id="answerTaxNote" class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 empty:hidden pt-1 border-t border-navy-200/60 dark:border-navy-800"></p>
  </div>

  <!--
    dd-017. Level premium purchasing power decay note
  -->
  <p id="answerPremiumDecay" class="mt-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed empty:hidden">
  </p>
</section>
